{"availability":"available","full":"0nlyl4/sign-language-translator","name":"sign-language-translator","description":"Real-time American Sign Language translator using MediaPipe hand tracking","category":"Developer tools","language":"HTML","stars":0,"forks":0,"created":"2026-08-16T20:17:13Z","updated":"2026-09-22T18:59:29Z","branch":"main","homepage":null,"topics":[],"demo":"https://0nlyl4.github.io/sign-language-translator/","color":"#193546","ink":"#a6d6e8","lastCheckedAt":"2026-10-04T08:16:53.532Z","lastAttemptAt":"2026-10-04T08:16:53.532Z","lastAvailableAt":"2026-10-04T08:16:53.532Z","metrics":[{"at":"2026-10-03T22:13:07.015Z","stars":0,"forks":0},{"at":"2026-10-04T08:16:53.532Z","stars":0,"forks":0}],"technologies":[],"overview":{"kind":"readme","version":1,"paragraphs":["Real-time recognition of 24 static American Sign Language (ASL) fingerspelling letters from webcam video. Trained on hand-landmark coordinates from MediaPipe, not raw images, achieving 97.17% accuracy across independent recording sessions.","This recognizes 24 static handshapes from the ASL fingerspelling alphabet. J and Z are excluded because they require motion, and a single static frame cannot capture a path. This is fingerspelling recognition, not full sign language translation.","This was built as a portfolio project to demonstrate end-to-end machine learning development: data collection, feature engineering, model training, evaluation, deployment, and documentation. The focus was on building a system that works in practice, measuring what matters (cross-session accuracy, not within-session), and documenting the experimental process honestly."],"features":["Real-time webcam recognition at camera frame rate","MediaPipe hand tracking extracts 21 3D joint positions (63 coordinates)","Normalized landmark features invariant to hand position and scale","Random Forest classifier with 100 decision trees","Confidence thresholding (60%) rejects ambiguous or unknown shapes"],"sourceUrl":"https://git